Native ACB production-source boundaries
======================================

run_native_data.py compiles the complete current native receive/send/take
bodies, DataCredits, NativeData and canonical LinkObservation. Signature-only
extern spelling is normalized for the balanced-body extractor. The allocation,
IRQ locks, owner registry, Backend, USB result and wakeup dependencies are
fixtures. No USB, kernel boot, ordinary-user socket or peer application runs.

Coverage: controller/generation/lifetime RX attribution, FIFO and packet limit,
non-consuming EMSGSIZE, actual credit reserve/NOCP release, zero/excess/unknown
completion, early completion before transport return, old debt at handle reuse,
uncertain transport, two-owner isolation and unchanged command-event delegation.
The production sources and fixture compile with warnings denied; no global
dead-code suppression is used. Existing atomic publisher tests cover lock order
and independent data wakeup even if the controller event log cannot publish.

The internal native ACB seam is not a userspace ioctl or a channel socket.
Legacy handle-only send remains unsupported. WS73 sends one full SDU using
queried size limits; Runtime owns packet credit, not the driver. Queued RX is
bounded to sixteen packets of at most 2047 bytes, tied to a canonical lifetime,
and retired at known link changes. No DTAP frame is parsed as a legacy raw PDU.

Still required: real A3/NOCP and peer receive evidence; CMTC capability/TCID/PI
handling; bounded socket TX queues, ordinary-user poll/close/link-down, real
bidirectional payload validation; fragmentation and data-flow fault gates.
NOCP lacks lifetime on the wire. Old debt blocks a reused handle until actual
matching counts settle it; neither Down nor USB success invents a credit.
Uncertain accounting stops data transmission for that controller generation.
It does not change command credit or reset another controller. Hardware debt
clearing and nonmanual recovery are unqualified; related issues remain open.

SDK111 real VM142 produced a 28-byte PB-first fragment for a 62-byte SDU,
despite queried TX capacity 254. RX now reassembles PB-first/middle/last per
registration/lifetime/handle, up to 2047 bytes, with a five-second absolute
deadline reclaimed by the per-controller heartbeat. First ingress reserves
the whole buffer outside IRQ locks; continuation cannot grow beyond it.
Expired, oversized, orphan or old-lifetime fragments never become complete
data or return a TX credit. Valid PB/priority flags are not USB/device faults.

RX readiness is published only after a complete PDU enters the bounded queue.
PB first/middle and orphan, expired, unknown-link or full-queue drops do not
wake Session TX retries. A partial input cannot clear a pending data/credit
hint. Real NOCP and lifecycle notifications keep their separate paths.
The production-function fixture counts actual ingress notification calls,
checks successful reassembly, queue-full/resume, original expiry and peer
isolation. Its RefCell locks, allocations, clock and counters remain explicit
dependencies, not kernel wakeup/concurrency or hardware/performance proof.
